# gitbackup hard-exclude list.
#
# One glob pattern per line, matched against an absolute path -- either one
# `sysupgrade -l` reported or a directory found while looking for empty
# directories (collect.sh, _gb_collect_is_excluded). "#" starts a comment,
# blank lines are ignored. A pattern ending in "/**" matches the path
# itself and everything under it; anything else is matched with plain
# shell `case` glob rules.
#
# This block is unconditional: it is enforced at every visibility setting.
# The extra secret-file excludes visibility=public adds (/etc/shadow,
# dropbear host keys, uhttpd.key, /etc/ssl/private/**, WireGuard private
# keys -- spec "Scrub") are not here: they depend on a UCI setting this
# file has no way to read, and belong to scrub.sh instead (ticket 05).

# The package's own credentials must never end up inside its own backup,
# no matter how the router is configured (R76).
/etc/gitbackup/**

# --- public-only hard-exclude (ticket 05, spec "Scrub") ---
#
# Every line below starting "#public:" is, to collect.sh, just another
# comment -- '#'*  is always skipped by _gb_collect_is_excluded, so
# collect.sh's own unconditional read of this file (used at every
# visibility, private included) never sees these as patterns and its
# behavior needs no visibility awareness added to it. scrub.sh is the only
# reader that treats "#public:" specially: it strips the prefix and applies
# what remains as a hard-exclude pattern against the already-collected tree,
# and scrub.sh itself only ever runs when visibility=public -- so the
# distinction lives in who reads the line, not in a flag either reader has
# to check. These are files no redaction can make safe to publish, because
# the secret IS the file, not a value inside it: removed from the tree
# entirely and dropped from manifest.json's entries[] (never scrubbed[] --
# that array is for redacted option values, not removed files). Same glob
# rules as above, applied to the pattern text after "#public:".
#
# Named verbatim in the brief and spec "Scrub":
#public: /etc/shadow
#public: /etc/dropbear/*_host_key*
#public: /etc/uhttpd.key
#public: /etc/ssl/private/**
# VERIFY: no OpenWrt-specific source confirms this path is ever populated --
# the wireguard package itself keeps keys inline in UCI (network.*.private_key,
# already in scrub.list) and has no file-based key storage of its own. This
# is the upstream wg-quick convention (a *.conf/key pair under
# /etc/wireguard/), kept as defense-in-depth for a router where someone
# generated keys by hand (`wg genkey`) and left the file there instead of
# only pasting it into UCI -- not verified against an OpenWrt package that
# actually writes there.
#public: /etc/wireguard/**
